{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `extractCitedThreadIds` regex is GitHub-specific and can truncate valid thread ids**

The regex `/PRRT_[A-Za-z0-9_]+/g` has two gaps in the exact mechanism this PR exists to close:

1. **Base64url `-` is missing from the character class.** GitHub compact GraphQL node ids are base64url (`A-Za-z0-9_-`); the live sample ids (`PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc6opl_v`) only happen to contain `_`, but an id containing `-` (e.g. `PRRT_kwDO…-w`) gets truncated at the `-`. The truncated id then fails both resolution paths for that thread: the fresh sweep compares `cited.indexOf(String(t.threadId))` against the *full* API id (no match), and the direct fallback calls `resolveThread` with the truncated id (GraphQL error, warn logged). The thread stays open → the unresolved-threads rule re-arms rework → the gh-692 loop persists for that thread.
2. **GitLab/ADO ids never match at all.** `instructions/pr_rework/output_rules.md` explicitly states the threadId "format depends on which SCM the PR lives on (a GitHub GraphQL node ID, a GitLab discussion hash, an ADO thread number, etc.)". On non-GitHub SCMs the MUST-resolve citation fallback is silently a no-op.

Suggested fix — go SCM-agnostic instead of pattern-matching the id format: you already load the setup-time snapshot in `postThreadReplies`, so resolve any snapshot threadId whose value appears in `responseText`:

```js
function extractCitedThreadIds(text, snapshotThreads) {
    var ids = [];
    var seen = {};
    String(text || '').replace(/PRRT_[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/g, function(m) {
        if (!seen[m]) { seen[m] = true; ids.push(m); }
        return m;
    });
    (snapshotThreads || []).forEach(function(t) {
        if (t && t.threadId && String(text || '').indexOf(String(t.threadId)) !== -1 &&
            ids.indexOf(String(t.threadId)) === -1) {
            ids.push(String(t.threadId));
        }
    });
    return ids;
}
```

That covers `-`-containing GitHub ids and GitLab/ADO ids alike. Failing that, at minimum add `-` to the class and document the GitHub-only scope of the citation fallback.
